WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.388 --> 00:00:09.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Deconstructing Chat Bots.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.260
I am Priyanka Vergadia
and in today's episode,

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.560
I have a very special guest
from Conversation Design team

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.975
at Google, Cathy Pearl.

00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:18.350
Thank you, Cathy,
for being here.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.920
CATHY PEARL: Thanks
so much for having me.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.462
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, Cathy,
so what is conversation design?

00:00:22.462 --> 00:00:23.888
What is your role at Google?

00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:26.430
CATHY PEARL: So I'm the Head of
Conversation Design Outreach,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.347
which is a bit of a
mouthful, but basically it

00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:30.830
means I tell people
both inside Google

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.350
and outside in the world
about conversation design,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.242
what it is, and
why it's important.

00:00:35.242 --> 00:00:37.700
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So what the
heck is conversation design?

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:38.390
CATHY PEARL: At
a high level, you

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.640
can think of it as
we're teaching computers

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.700
to communicate more like humans
and not the other way around.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.180
And you know, a lot of us have
had frustrating experiences

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.540
with our voices systems.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.390
I don't know if you've ever
had a frustrating experience.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.807
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
A ton of times.

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:52.700
How do we avoid them?

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.570
CATHY PEARL: A lot
of times, people

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.030
think it's a technical
problem, but in fact, it's

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.012
often because design best
practices were not followed.

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:01.220
So we want to make sure that
when people are building

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.980
these things, if you follow
good design principles,

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.720
you're much more likely to
have a successful experience.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
I am a developer,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.540
so I really don't
write design things.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.800
Where do I get started?

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.850
CATHY PEARL: We
always recommend you

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.642
start with something
pretty straightforward

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:16.910
called a sample dialogue.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.680
It's basically like
a movie script.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.250
It's a back and forth between
your user and your system.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.860
And it really helps you
kind of flesh out your ideas

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.230
and make sure you're going
in the right direction.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.880
And you just write these down.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.710
And I always tell
people, don't worry.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.960
They really don't have to
be perfect, especially right

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:33.440
out of the gate.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.030
Mine are certainly not perfect
when I first write them down.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.550
But over time, just
remember that old rule,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.270
there's no great writing,
there's only great rewriting.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.150
And after you've
written some, you

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.430
do just like you do in
Hollywood with a movie script,

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:47.720
you do a table read.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:48.710
You read them out loud.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.990
So you find a willing
victim, and then you

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.490
have them play the
part of the user.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.310
And you just take
turns reading out loud.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.310
And you'll be surprised.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.830
You'll actually find a
lot of problems and issues

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.680
with your design just through
that alone, before you've

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.300
written a single line of code.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So, Cathy, I know

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.460
how to have a conversation.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.270
Why do I have to
design a conversation

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.340
before I start working?

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.840
CATHY PEARL: I think
a lot of people

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.382
are used to designing for things
like mobile apps or websites

00:02:14.382 --> 00:02:16.400
where when somebody
taps a button

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.010
or swipes or selects a menu
item, you know what they chose.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.010
And if you're designing
a conversational system,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.500
whether it's voice
or typing, people

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.000
are going to say
all kinds of things

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.440
that you're not going expect.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.480
And part of conversation
design is really

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.383
guiding people to
success by showing them

00:02:33.383 --> 00:02:35.550
the types of things they're
going to be able to say,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.265
in order for you to
be able to respond.

00:02:38.265 --> 00:02:40.640
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, so I
get that I need to learn more

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.550
about conversation design,
but if I write my prompts

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:47.030
correctly, then do you think
everything else will just

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:47.695
follow?

00:02:47.695 --> 00:02:49.070
CATHY PEARL: The
better you write

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.560
your prompts and your
flow, the more likely

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.600
you are going to get
answers you can respond to.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.460
But the big thing I
always tell people

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.220
is when you're working with
conversational experiences,

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.130
people will always say
things you didn't expect.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.660
And that's just kind of human.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.730
I mean, when we're
talking, we often

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.425
don't understand
every single word

00:03:06.425 --> 00:03:07.550
that the other person says.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.500
But we don't say
oh, we had errors.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.750
That's because we're so
good at what we call repair.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.850
We repair our conversations
very gracefully

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.660
and we get back on track.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.780
So that's the kind
of strategy you

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.760
want to apply to your
conversational systems.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.380
If you can repair
things gracefully

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.090
when people say things
you don't expect,

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.030
because that will happen, then
you can get them back on track

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:26.960
and keep going.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:27.710
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Can you walk me

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.690
through some of the
examples of how you

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.392
would repair a conversation?

00:03:31.392 --> 00:03:33.350
CATHY PEARL: Yeah, so if
somebody says things--

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.720
says something your
system didn't expect,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.200
one quick thing is just to
rephrase the options that are

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.730
available at that current time.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.688
You definitely don't want
to say like, I didn't

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.278
understand, or say that again.

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:45.820
You're not helping
the person, right?

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.653
You're trying to get
through your experience

00:03:47.653 --> 00:03:49.490
and you're just,
you're abandoning them.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.640
So one way is to just sort of
rephrase the current options.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.530
Not all the things
they can possibly

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:57.260
do, but just like, right now
what should they be focused on?

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.830
Another part of that is
called rapid reprompt.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.220
So that's just a really
fast way to do that.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.760
So let's say you're writing
a chocolate recipe bot,

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.900
and you ask, do you like milk
chocolate or dark chocolate?

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.980
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Milk chocolate.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.490
CATHY PEARL: And the person
says, 65%, which maybe we

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:14.700
don't understand that.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.003
So a rapid reprompt would
be, sorry, was that milk

00:04:17.003 --> 00:04:18.170
chocolate or dark chocolate?

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:19.970
And that will get
people back on track.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.220
And one other thing I want
to say about error handling

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.160
or repair is a lot of
time, that first rephrasing

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.920
or rapid reprompt will
get people right back on

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.310
and going again, but if they
have a second error right away,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.100
there's probably not
enough information

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.277
for them to know what
to do or what to say.

00:04:36.277 --> 00:04:38.360
So at that point, you might
want to have something

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.390
a little bit longer with a
little more detail, like oh,

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.637
your account number is
on the upper left hand

00:04:43.637 --> 00:04:45.470
corner of your bill,
or something like that.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.300
Because they're struggling
at that point and you just

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.310
really want to help them out.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.310
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK,
so what I'm understanding

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.670
is don't just say, I
didn't get that, right.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.580
Just try to be a little
bit more specific

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.250
about what was it that
you were expecting

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.080
and how can the user
get back on track.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.320
CATHY PEARL: Exactly.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.737
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That's actually

00:05:02.737 --> 00:05:04.380
very interesting
and amazing advice.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.330
We are seeing a lot of smart
displays coming into being.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.000
What are your
recommendations for designing

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.500
for those smart displays?

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.730
CATHY PEARL: Yeah, I'm
really excited to see

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.100
some of these different ways
that people can interact.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.808
And I think smart
displays in particular,

00:05:18.808 --> 00:05:20.767
there's going to be a
lot of interesting things

00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:22.380
that people come up with.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.330
What we advise is
to always start

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.460
designing the voice-only
experience first,

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.520
which might seem a
little counterintuitive.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.370
But in fact, the
voice-only experience

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.810
is often the most
constrained and complex.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:34.980
I'll give you an example.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.730
Let's say you're just
writing about business hours.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.160
And if you're thinking about
a mobile phone experience,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.615
if I'm asking you, you know,
what time is Sushi King open?

00:05:43.615 --> 00:05:45.240
You can just pop up
a card on the phone

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.420
and it has, Monday 10 to 6,
Tuesday 11 to 7, or whatever.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.110
But if I just ask on a
smart speaker and I say,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.450
what time is it open today?

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.150
If you started saying, Monday
it's open 6:00 AM to 7:00 PM.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.120
Tuesday it's open--

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.720
I mean, that's just like
ugh, way too much, right?

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.780
So instead, you want
to craft the prompt

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.950
just like a human would,
to answer just the question

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:05.450
itself.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.260
So if I say, is
it open right now?

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.560
That's a yes/no question.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:09.910
You should start with yes or no.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.720
If I say, you know, what time
does it open on Wednesday?

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.310
It opens on Wednesday at 11:00.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.850
And you'll find you might have
to write dozens of prompts

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:22.873
to handle that, when on a mobile
phone, it's only one card.

00:06:22.873 --> 00:06:23.790
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.310
What would be some of your
final pieces of advice

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:27.690
for our viewers today?

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:29.065
CATHY PEARL: Well,
first I'm just

00:06:29.065 --> 00:06:31.082
really excited to see
what kind of experiences

00:06:31.082 --> 00:06:32.790
you all come up with
that are going to be

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.010
really interesting to see.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.890
I also think that voice
has really matured

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.900
in the accessibility realm.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.730
I think we're finally
seeing examples

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.380
where voice
technology can really

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.690
help people who might
have motor impairments

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.410
or visual impairments or
even cognitive impairments.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.930
And I think this just
brings us into the idea

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.890
of inclusive design, which
means we're not just designing

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.140
for this person or
that person, but really

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.310
designing for all of us.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.050
I mean, if you think
about it, we all

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.030
have impairments
from time to time.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.940
Situational impairments, like,
I'm carrying the groceries in

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.157
and I can't use my hands, or
I forgot my reading glasses

00:07:09.157 --> 00:07:10.990
and I really want to
read this text message.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:13.157
So being able to use my
voice is really handy there.

00:07:13.157 --> 00:07:15.580
Or maybe I fall and break my
arm or something like that.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.540
So I'm just excited to see that
voice technology has really

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.660
risen more to the
challenge of helping people

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.120
who really need it the most.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.120
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:24.780
Thanks, Cathy.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.780
This was awesome.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.293
I really learned a lot about
conversation design today.

00:07:28.293 --> 00:07:29.710
CATHY PEARL: Thanks
for having me.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:30.450
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Thank you, everybody,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.750
for watching this show.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.710
I hope that you guys liked
Cathy's recommendations

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.930
and apply some of those in
your conversation designs.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.030
And if you would
like to learn more,

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:43.680
go to actions.google.com/design.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.870
Well, I didn't design the sample
dialogue for this conversation,

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.205
so we have to do take two.

00:07:49.205 --> 00:07:50.205
CATHY PEARL: Take three.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.310
That's a wrap.

